  <h1>Disclaimer</h1>
  <p class="lede">Last updated: October 10, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>1. Educational purpose only</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub is an educational reference about Chinese contemplative traditions. All
    content — articles, translations, data records and practitioner reports — is provided
    for general information and scholarly interest only. It is not advice of any kind and
    is not tailored to any individual.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Not medical advice</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing on these sites is intended to diagnose, treat, cure or prevent any disease or
    condition. Discussion of meditation research reports what studies show and what they
    do not prove; it is never a medical endorsement. Do not start, change or stop any
    treatment based on anything you read here. Consult a qualified healthcare professional
    about medical matters.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Not mental-health advice</h2>
  <p>
    Meditation is not a substitute for psychotherapy or psychiatric care. If you are
    experiencing distress, depression, anxiety or altered states that concern you, seek
    help from a qualified mental-health professional. Descriptions of contemplative
    practice on this site are historical and ethnographic, not therapeutic guidance.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Not professional advice</h2>
  <p>
    This site does not provide legal, financial, religious-instructional or teaching
    credentials. No teacher-student or practitioner-client relationship is created by
    reading this site.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Practice safety</h2>
  <p>
    Some traditional practices described on this site involve physical or psychological
    intensity — prolonged sitting, breath regulation or retention, fasting, sleep
    restriction and intensive retreat formats. These descriptions are documentary, not
    instructions. If you choose to explore any practice:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Consult a physician first if you are pregnant, or have cardiovascular,
      respiratory, neurological or psychiatric conditions;</li>
    <li>Stop any practice that causes pain, breathlessness, fainting or distress;</li>
    <li>Learn under qualified, accountable instruction where available;</li>
    <li>Be aware that meditation can in some individuals surface or intensify
      psychological difficulties — treat your own stability as the priority.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Historical and traditional content</h2>
  <p>
    We describe traditions as they are recorded — including claims made within those
    traditions — without asserting their truth, safety or applicability today. Legend is
    presented as legend, hagiography as hagiography, and modern claims are flagged with
    their evidence class. See the
    <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/evidence-system/">Evidence System</a> for
    the classification used throughout.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Accuracy and completeness</h2>
  <p>
    We work to keep content accurate, sourced and dated, but we do not warrant that the
    sites are free of errors or omissions. Classical-text translations vary across
    scholarly traditions; ours are one documented rendering among several. Where evidence
    is thin, the site says so explicitly. Verify against primary and scholarly sources
    before relying on any statement.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Practitioner experience records</h2>
  <p>
    The experience collection presents anonymized self-reports gathered from public
    community discussions. They are individual accounts, not evidence of benefit or
    safety, and they are not representative of any population. No conclusions about
    efficacy should be drawn from them.
  </p>

  <h2>9. External links</h2>
  <p>
    Links to external sites (such as ctext.org) are citations of record. We are not
    responsible for the content, accuracy or policies of third-party sites, nor does a
    link imply endorsement.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Limitation of liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the fullest extent permitted by applicable law, DaoismHub and its operator shall
